WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.312
Here's the core problem.

2
00:00:01.312 --> 00:00:07.562
A PC joined only to Microsoft Entra ID
still tries to register its name in DNS.

3
00:00:07.562 --> 00:00:08.574
What happens?

4
00:00:08.574 --> 00:00:11.930
By default, Windows tries
an unsigned update first,

5
00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:15.125
and only if that's refused
does it try a secure one.

6
00:00:15.125 --> 00:00:18.129
A zone that is set to
accept only secure updates

7
00:00:18.129 --> 00:00:20.825
refuses the unsigned
attempt, as it should.

8
00:00:20.825 --> 00:00:25.281
Then the secure attempt: Microsoft
documents that Windows dynamic

9
00:00:25.281 --> 00:00:29.975
update clients and DNS servers can
use only Kerberos for secure update.

10
00:00:29.975 --> 00:00:34.410
As an inference, an Entra-joined PC
has no machine account in the domain,

11
00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:38.463
so it can't sign, and the zone gets
no record from that client at all.

12
00:00:38.463 --> 00:00:42.174
The tempting fix is to let the
zone accept unsigned updates.

13
00:00:42.174 --> 00:00:42.863
Don't.

14
00:00:42.863 --> 00:00:45.494
Microsoft says that without secure update,

15
00:00:45.494 --> 00:00:49.149
any computer on the network can
modify the existing A record.

16
00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:50.225
More facts.

17
00:00:50.225 --> 00:00:54.537
Over a VPN, Windows doesn't
attempt dynamic update by default.

18
00:00:54.537 --> 00:01:00.220
Since the May 2020 Windows 10 update, it
also skips servers named in an NRPT rule,

19
00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:06.251
the Name Resolution Policy Table, which
sends certain names to certain DNS servers.

20
00:01:06.251 --> 00:01:09.029
And Microsoft's advice
to let clients register

21
00:01:09.029 --> 00:01:11.950
their own records assumes
domain-joined clients.

22
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:14.312
As an inference, it can't apply here.

23
00:01:14.312 --> 00:01:17.924
So something else has to register
endpoints, and it has to sign.

24
00:01:17.924 --> 00:01:24.677
Sources: Microsoft Learn: Dynamic
update (Windows Server DNS) [S26];

25
00:01:24.677 --> 00:01:27.862
Policy CSP ADMX_DnsClient [S27];

26
00:01:27.862 --> 00:01:36.399
